{
  "configuration_id": "config_040",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_020 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) is the best result so far at 0.639 acc / 0.620 F1, clearly outperforming the RNA-only baseline (config_007, 0.591) and all scgpt-based configs. The fusion method (weighted vs concat) is the one design axis not yet varied around this best configuration. Question: config_040 changes only the fusion from weighted to concat while holding model_setting, profile_setting, both encoders, and aggregation identical to config_020, giving a clean single-variable test of whether a concatenation-based fusion (letting the classifier learn cross-modal interactions) outperforms a learned weighted sum. Why this is the best next step: under a tight budget, isolating one variable against the current best is the most information-efficient choice. If concat improves on weighted, it identifies a concrete lever to push performance higher; if it does not, we can rule out fusion as a productive direction and redirect budget toward image-encoder or aggregation choices with confidence, rather than spending experiments on a less-informative multi-variable change."
}

